The board is now stable: `heap_free` is flat at 391916 bytes, `sent` climbs monotonically, `failed=0`, `queue=0`. The `send failed ... retrying` warnings are the expected normal behavior (the simulated radio is busy every 5th call, and the retry then succeeds).

## Root cause

`send_with_retry()` in `main/main.c` leaked memory on every failed send. The contract in `link.h` is clear: `link_send()` only takes ownership of `pkt.data` on `ESP_OK`; on any error the caller keeps ownership. But the loop allocated a fresh encoded buffer each attempt and, on failure, simply looped around without freeing the previous one:

```c
pkt.data = encode_frame(frame, len, &pkt.len);
esp_err_t err = link_send(&pkt);
if (err == ESP_OK) return ESP_OK;
/* pkt.data leaked here */
```

The simulated radio returns `ESP_ERR_TIMEOUT` on every 5th call, so each frame leaked roughly 1–3 buffers of `len + 8` = 4104 bytes. At 50 Hz that is tens of KB per second. Once the heap was exhausted, `encode_frame()` returned `NULL`, every send failed (`ESP_ERR_NO_MEM`), and shortly after `build_frame()`'s `malloc` failed, hitting `abort()` — "every send fails and nothing gets through."

## Fix

In `main/main.c`, free the encoded buffer before retrying when `link_send()` reports an error (the last failed attempt is covered too, since each iteration frees its own buffer):

```c
esp_err_t err = link_send(&pkt);
if (err == ESP_OK) {
    return ESP_OK;
}
/* link_send() only takes ownership of pkt.data on success; free it here
 * so failed attempts don't leak the encoded buffer. */
free(pkt.data);
ESP_LOGW(TAG, "send failed (%s), retrying", esp_err_to_name(err));
retries++;
```

No changes to `link.c`/`link.h`, the frame size, or the 50 Hz rate. Built and flashed to `/dev/cu.usbmodem101`; verified over 30+ seconds of runtime with a steady heap and continuous successful sends.
